            text: "Step on at the machine's lowest speed while holding on, then most seniors walk at about 1 to 1.5 mph for the first sessions and build toward a comfortable 2 to 3 mph. Frail or recovering walkers may stay below 2 mph, which is where a 0.3 mph start and 0.1 mph steps help. Our treadmill speed for seniors guide covers typical speeds by age.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can a senior use a walking pad without a handrail?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Only if they walk confidently without support, and even then a pad with a handlebar is the better buy for starting and stopping. A pad with no rail leaves nothing to hold during a stumble, on a belt that is narrower than a treadmill's. Anyone unsure should ask a doctor or physical therapist first.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#shaheer-ali-khan", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", honorificSuffix: "PhD", jobTitle: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist", url: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#who", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
