        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Is a walking pad safe for seniors?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It can be for a senior who walks steadily without support, chooses a pad with a handlebar, starts at the lowest speed and always uses the safety key or auto-stop. It is not the safer choice for someone who needs to hold on while walking, because most pads have no rail and those that do have only a short front bar or small side handles.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a treadmill or a walking pad better for an elderly person?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most elderly walkers a treadmill with full-length side rails is better, because they can keep a hand on support for the whole walk and many start at very slow speeds, such as 0.3 mph on the Exerpeutic Senior Fitness Treadmill. A walking pad is better only when space is very tight and the walker is steady on their feet.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do walking pads have handrails for seniors?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Some do. The Sunny Treadpad has a front handlebar, and some WalkingPad models have short side handles or a removable bar. None has the full-length rails of a senior treadmill. If a low step and full rails are both needed, a low-deck treadmill with extended rails is the closer fit.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the lowest step-up height on a walking machine?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Walking pads and low-deck treadmills sit around 4 inches off the floor. Sunny gives 4.1 inches for its Treadpad lying flat and 4 inches floor to deck for its Low Deck treadmill. Conventional treadmills are roughly 7 to 12 inches. Incline raises the step, so return it to zero before getting on or off.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What speed should a senior start at on a treadmill or walking pad?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Step on at the machine's lowest speed while holding on, then most seniors walk at about 1 to 1.5 mph for the first sessions and build toward a comfortable 2 to 3 mph. Frail or recovering walkers may stay below 2 mph, which is where a 0.3 mph start and 0.1 mph steps help. Our treadmill speed for seniors guide covers typical speeds by age.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can a senior use a walking pad without a handrail?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Only if they walk confidently without support, and even then a pad with a handlebar is the better buy for starting and stopping. A pad with no rail leaves nothing to hold during a stumble, on a belt that is narrower than a treadmill's. Anyone unsure should ask a doctor or physical therapist first.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

